<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.ztomer.goh-load-reap</string>

    <!-- The load backstop (2026-10-20). A load experiment's spinners are registered in the load
         registry (GOH_LOAD_DIR, default ~/.cache/goh/load) by $GOH_DIR/tools/load.py, which kills
         them on the way out. A trap cannot catch an orphan: the tool itself was SIGKILLed, three
         times in one day, and the spinners were re-parented to launchd and burned a core each for
         hours (24 of them for 3h12m, load average 206). So the machine reads the registry every
         minute and kills the pids in it whose parent is 1. A spinner with a LIVE parent is an
         experiment in progress and is left alone; nothing is ever killed by name.

         THE SECOND SHAPE (later the same day). A record can VANISH: the registry of an
         experiment that ran in a temporary directory is pruned with that directory, and its
         spinner is left burning a core with nothing naming it -- eight of them, orphaned by a
         pytest that died, about an hour of CPU apiece. So the job also recognises the house's
         own spinner shape -- EXACTLY that shape, orphaned only, and only while the owner its
         release pipe names is gone. That needs a name lookup, which is the RECORDED EXEMPTION in
         kill_by_name_allow.json (the house forbids killing by name): the lookup and the kill sit
         on one line, so the rule sees them, and every candidate is matched exactly before it is
         touched.

         THE THIRD SHAPE (2026-10-21). A spinner need not be the house's at all: twenty-four
         hand-rolled ones were found burning four cores each for four and a half hours, and the
         second shape knew only ONE command line, so it left every one of them alone. So the job
         asks a shape it can NAME without owning it: an ORPHAN (parent 1) that has BURNED a core
         (its CPU time is over the floor) and is STILL burning, on a busy loop (the code a shell
         runs contains one). Four questions, and none of them alone is enough -- a live parent, a
         sleeper, a worker that has finished, and a program that is not a shell are all left
         alone. That needs a name lookup too, so it is the SECOND RECORDED EXEMPTION, with the
         same shape: the lookup and the kill on one line, and every candidate read back and asked
         its questions before it is touched. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <!-- path-ok: launchd execs this with no shell and no environment, so an absolute path is the only form it can use. The guard is quiet until the gates checkout carries the reaper (this repo), so a job installed before the branch lands says nothing every minute instead of failing every minute. -->
        <string>p=/Users/ztomer/Projects/gates_of_heck/tools/load_reap.sh; [ -x "$p" ] || exit 0; exec "$p"</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <!-- path-ok: the gates checkout, so the job resolves the house interpreter and the HEAD export cache. -->
    <string>/Users/ztomer/Projects/gates_of_heck</string>

    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <!-- path-ok: launchd opens this file itself and does not expand `~`, so the path must be absolute. -->
    <string>/Users/ztomer/Library/Logs/goh-load-reap.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <!-- path-ok: the same file as StandardOutPath, for the same reason. -->
    <string>/Users/ztomer/Library/Logs/goh-load-reap.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>NO_COLOR</key><string>1</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
